1
00:00:01,207 --> 00:00:09,776
Welcome to Africa episode five of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,428 --> 00:00:28,680
Six countries connect the Nile with the Horn: Sudan, South Sudan, Eritrea,
Djibouti, Ethiopia and Somalia. Somaliland is demonstrated within Somalia’s
stop, with its claimed independence explained separately.

3
00:00:29,361 --> 00:00:32,635
Let's bring Sudan and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:36,681 --> 00:00:47,896
Sudan lies below Egypt, east of Chad and north of South Sudan. Its
northeastern coast reaches the Red Sea, while the Nile crosses its interior.

5
00:00:48,560 --> 00:01:02,805
Gebel Barkal and nearby archaeological sites preserve temples, tombs and
pyramids associated with the kingdom of Kush. Pyramids are part of Sudan’s
history as well as Egypt’s.

6
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:11,819
Picture Kushite pyramids farther upstream from Egypt. Sudan is north of South
Sudan and still has a Red Sea coast.

7
00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:25,760
South Sudan is landlocked below Sudan, west of Ethiopia and north of Uganda
and Kenya. The White Nile passes through its interior.

8
00:01:26,422 --> 00:01:42,885
The Sudd is an immense wetland associated with the White Nile, with channels,
marshes and seasonally flooded grasslands. A great river can spread into a
broad living landscape rather than remain one narrow line.

9
00:01:43,536 --> 00:01:53,045
Picture the Nile spreading into marshes. South Sudan is the inland country
below Sudan; it has no Red Sea coast.

10
00:01:53,736 --> 00:01:57,532
Let's bring Eritrea and its neighbours into view.

11
00:02:00,877 --> 00:02:09,143
Eritrea follows the Red Sea coast east of Sudan and north of Ethiopia.
Djibouti lies at its southeastern end.

12
00:02:09,804 --> 00:02:24,734
Asmara, the highland capital, preserves a remarkable collection of early
modernist buildings developed during Italian colonial rule. Cinemas, shops and
public buildings form a distinctive urban landscape.

13
00:02:25,391 --> 00:02:35,759
Picture modernist buildings above a long Red Sea coastline. Eritrea separates
much of northern Ethiopia from the sea.

14
00:02:38,349 --> 00:02:50,714
Djibouti is the small country at the meeting of the Red Sea approach and the
Gulf of Aden. Eritrea is north, Ethiopia west, and Somalia’s northern region
southeast.

15
00:02:51,368 --> 00:03:04,081
Lake Assal occupies a depression below sea level and is famous for very salty
water and salt deposits. The rift landscape reminds us that this region sits
near separating tectonic plates.

16
00:03:04,741 --> 00:03:15,236
Picture a salt lake beside the Horn’s narrow sea gateway. Djibouti is small
and coastal; Ethiopia beside it is much larger and landlocked.

17
00:03:15,944 --> 00:03:19,485
Let's bring Ethiopia and its neighbours into view.

18
00:03:23,510 --> 00:03:35,875
Ethiopia is the large inland highland country west of Somalia and south of
Eritrea. Sudan and South Sudan lie west, while Kenya is south.

19
00:03:36,526 --> 00:03:47,358
At Lalibela, medieval churches were cut directly into the rock. Some stand
inside excavated trenches, so the surrounding stone was removed to reveal the
building’s shape.

20
00:03:48,013 --> 00:03:59,228
Picture churches carved downward into a highland landscape. Ethiopia is inland
behind Eritrea and Djibouti; it does not have a coast.

21
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:02,939
Let's bring Somalia and its neighbours into view.

22
00:04:05,701 --> 00:04:19,865
Somalia forms the eastern Horn, with a long Indian Ocean coast and a northern
shore on the Gulf of Aden. Ethiopia lies inland to its west and Kenya to its
southwest.

23
00:04:20,696 --> 00:04:34,640
In the northwestern Somaliland region, Laas Geel preserves prehistoric
paintings of cattle and people in rock shelters. Picture those painted herds
near the upper side of the Horn, above inland Ethiopia.

24
00:04:35,299 --> 00:04:52,663
GeoClick also separates Somaliland in the northwest, which we show here as a
related target. Its authorities claim independence; the simplified outline
does not settle recognition, sovereignty or territorial control.

25
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:17,086
Sudan lies below Egypt, with South Sudan below it. Eritrea follows the Red
Sea, tiny Djibouti sits at the Gulf of Aden gateway, Ethiopia is inland, and
Somalia forms the eastern Horn.

26
00:05:17,745 --> 00:05:34,881
Recall Kushite pyramids, Nile wetlands, modernist Asmara, the salt lake,
rock-cut churches and painted cattle near the Horn. GeoClick’s separate
Somaliland target is a related selection, not another country counted in this
series.
